  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\int_{1}^{b} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^2 - 1}}\\,dt \\;=\\; \\operatorname{arcosh} b \\;=\\; \\log\\!\\left(b + \\sqrt{b^2 - 1}\\right), \\qquad b \\ge 1,",
    "plain": "The parent entry proves the **antiderivative** identity $\\int 1/\\sqrt{t^2-1}\\,dt = \\operatorname{arcosh} t + C$ on the open interval $(1, \\infty)$. This leaf upgrades that to a genuine **definite (improper) integral** from $1$ to $b$: the integrand blows up at the lower endpoint $t = 1$, so the integral is improper, but it converges because $1/\\sqrt{t^2-1} \\sim 1/\\sqrt{2(t-1)}$ is integrable there. The value is exactly $\\operatorname{arcosh} b$.",
